<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Vorster</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1915">1915</a>–<a href="/years/1983">1983</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as B. J. Vorster, Balthazar Johannes Vorster</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Vorster was a South African politician who was prime minister from 1966 to 1978 and then state president until 1979. As minister of justice under Verwoerd he oversaw harsh security laws against the anti-apartheid movement. As prime minister he pursued détente with African states, while his government faced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soweto-uprising">Soweto uprising</a> of 1976; he resigned after the Information Scandal.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-vorster.jpg" alt="John Vorster" width="320" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John-Vorster_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Balthazar Johannes Vorster was born on 13 December 1915 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamestown">Jamestown</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-province">Cape Province</a>, the fifteenth child of a successful sheep farmer. At Stellenbosch University he became chairman of the debating society, deputy chairman of the student council and leader of the junior <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a>. He graduated in 1938 and opened law practices at Port Elizabeth and Brakpan.</p><p>From 1939 he strongly opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>'s entry into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> on the side of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>, and joined the Ossewabrandwag, an anti-British, pro-Nazi organisation that carried out acts of sabotage during the war. He became a general in its paramilitary wing and was detained at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koffiefontein">Koffiefontein</a> in 1942; he denied taking part in its violence and said he was interned for being anti-British. After his release in 1944 he became active in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a> and entered the House of Assembly for Nigel in 1953.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Verwoerd appointed him Minister of Justice in 1961, adding Police and Prisons in 1966. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rivonia-trial">Rivonia Trial</a>, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela">Nelson Mandela</a> was sentenced to life imprisonment for sabotage, came under him as Minister of Justice. After Verwoerd was assassinated in 1966 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a> elected Vorster to succeed him. He continued apartheid legislation, including the abolition of the last four parliamentary seats for white representatives of Coloured voters, but dropped several proposed bills and repealed the ban on multiracial sports teams so that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> could attempt to compete at the 1968 Olympics; the IOC refused the team permission. The Terrorism Act and the Soweto Riots fell in his years as prime minister.</p><p>In foreign policy he was more pragmatic than his predecessors, allowing Black African diplomats to live in white areas. After Portuguese rule collapsed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a> in 1975 he pressed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ian-smith">Ian Smith</a> of Rhodesia, in September 1976 under US pressure from Henry Kissinger, to accept in principle that white minority rule could not last. After the Soweto Uprising he let the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of Information carry out secret activities financed from a secret defence account without telling his cabinet; the auditor general's critical report led to a scandal, and he resigned as prime minister in 1978.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Vorster then became State President, a largely honorary post, but a commission of inquiry concluded in mid-1979 that he had known everything about the Muldergate corruption, including a slush fund used to found The Citizen newspaper, and he resigned. He died in September 1983, aged 67, of a blood clot in the lung after a lung infection and emphysema. A building at Stellenbosch University, built on land taken from coloured residents under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/group-areas-act">Group Areas Act</a>, was named for him and renamed in the 1990s.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John Vorster” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Verwoerd (1958–1966), <b>John Vorster</b> (1966–1978) and P. W. Botha (1978–1989) led National Party governments that ruled through security laws, detention without trial and the banning of opponents.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-south-africa">Republic of South Africa · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…gave covert support and a channel through Mozambique, and the three governments shared intelligence under the Alcora Exercise, but after the Carnation Revolution of 1974 Portugal downgraded its mission and <b>John Vorster</b>, pursuing détente with black African states, held up fuel and ammunition and withdrew South African forces.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rhodesia">Rhodesia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Uitenhage, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-33.75,25.4&amp;year=1959" aria-label="Uitenhage on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5198 3387 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of John Vorster"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5648 3657h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5648 3657h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-33.75,25.4&amp;year=1959">What was here in 1959? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
